course: dictionary
lesson: 7202
title: Adverbs that show feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An adverb tells you how something is done. In «"I don't care!" she said pettishly», the word pettishly tells you how she spoke. Use the clues around it: "I don't care!" sounds cross and stubborn, so pettishly fits.
explain: Pettishly means in a cross, sulky, childish way, as when someone is easily annoyed about small things. When the Rat asks «Would you like to come over?», the Mole answers «rather pettishly» (a bit crossly) «Oh, it's all very well to talk», because he is new to the river and cannot see how to get across.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «She said sulkily» still makes sense, so sulky is close to pettishly, and you have the right idea.
fact: Pettishly is an adverb: it tells you how someone speaks or acts.
fact: «Pettishly» means in a cross, sulky, childish way.
fact: A pettish person is easily annoyed about small things, like a child who wants the last biscuit.
fact: «Pettishly» is close in meaning to «sulkily», but it adds a childish feel.
fact: Lewis Carroll uses pettishly in Alice in Wonderland for grumpy characters, and for Alice when she is fed up.
exercise: «"I don't care!" she said pettishly.» How did she say it? | Slowly, as if she was sleepy | *In a cross, sulky, childish way | In a calm and gentle voice | Very loudly and bravely
exercise: Ben stamped his foot and said, «It's not fair!» pettishly. What kind of mood is Ben in? | Very happy and excited | Calm and patient | *Easily annoyed and sulky, like a grumpy child | Brave and bold
exercise: «Fine, have it your way,» she said pettishly. Find and copy the word that tells you how she spoke. | said | *pettishly | way | Fine
test:
Which word is closest in meaning to pettishly? | *sulkily | happily | bravely | quietly
The toddler folded his arms and frowned pettishly because he could not have the last biscuit. Why did he frown? | *Because he was annoyed about a small thing | Because he had won a prize | Because he was very cold | Because it was his birthday
exam:
Which word means calmly? | pettishly | *composedly | gingerly | folly
